# Task 8. java.util.Random follows the algorithms its Javadoc states, and the seed behind new Random()
# and Math.random() comes from the host, once per Run (D16). Written from the public Javadoc (Random,
# RandomGenerator, Math.random) and the pinned JDK's observed output (clean room, D14; no OpenJDK source
# was opened), except the default-seed mixer, which follows SplitMix64 (its bullet below says from where)
# and is a design choice, not JDK behavior.
#   - TeaVM's Random ignored its seed: the seeded constructor and setSeed did nothing and every method drew
#     from Math.random(), so new Random(42) printed different numbers on every run. Now setSeed, next(bits),
#     nextInt(), nextInt(bound), nextLong, nextBoolean, nextFloat, nextDouble, nextGaussian and nextBytes
#     are the Javadoc's own code, with its exception message ("bound must be positive").
#   - nextInt(origin, bound) is inherited from RandomGenerator, whose Javadoc leaves the algorithm open. The
#     one here was chosen black-box: on 3,000 sequences (15 ranges, 200 seeds, 20 draws each, then one more
#     nextLong to count the draws consumed) it matched the JDK on all, where four rivals differed on 800 to
#     2,800 (plain nextInt(n) + origin differs on power-of-two ranges). It is the int form of the
#     nextLong(origin, bound) printed in Random's longs() Javadoc. The message is the JDK's ("bound must be
#     greater than origin").
#   - new Random() seeds itself from the host import teavmRandom.seed (a double: a whole number below
#     2^53), read once per program, then stepped once per new Random() and mixed (SplitMix64), so each
#     generator gets its own seed and a replay with the same host seed gets the same ones. The runner
#     answers the import from the Run's randomSeed, or with a fresh value when there is none.
#   - The default-seed mixer is a design choice, not JDK behavior: the JDK's default seed cannot be
#     observed (it differs on every run), so nothing here can match or miss it. The mixer is a Weyl step
#     plus the SplitMix64 finalizer (Steele, Lea and Flood, "Fast Splittable Pseudorandom Number
#     Generators", OOPSLA 2014; Vigna's public-domain splitmix64.c at prng.di.unimi.it). Its constants
#     (0x9E3779B97F4A7C15, 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9, 0x94D049BB133111EB, shifts 30, 27, 31) were first written
#     from recall, then checked against a copy of splitmix64.c fetched from prng.di.unimi.it: all match.
#   - Both constructors call setSeed, which a subclass may override, as Random(long)'s Javadoc states (new
#     Random(seed) is equivalent to new Random() followed by setSeed(seed)); the pinned JDK calls an
#     overriding setSeed once from each constructor (case R8-setseed-override). ThreadLocalRandom, whose
#     setSeed throws, passes that first call through to Random's until its own constructor has run, so
#     its static instance still builds and still takes its seed from the Run.
#   - Math.random() on Wasm GC draws nextDouble() from one new Random() made on first use, as its Javadoc
#     says, instead of calling JavaScript's Math.random. Other platforms are unchanged.
#   - Collections.shuffle, ThreadLocalRandom and SecureRandom extend or call TRandom and keep working;
#     ThreadLocalRandom and the one-argument shuffle now take their seed from the Run too, so they replay.
#     Collections.shuffle(list, new Random(s)) now prints the JDK's order (50 of 50 seeds).
# Known limits, seen and not fixed (outside this patch's list of methods):
#   - nextGaussian went through StrictMath.log, which TeaVM mapped to JavaScript's Math.log; that differed
#     from the JDK's StrictMath.log on 1.6% of inputs in (0, 1), so about 0.75% of Gaussian values differed
#     in the last digit (151 of 20,000 from one seed). Fixed since by teavm-0015, which ports fdlibm's log
#     (Task 11): case R11-gaussian is now identical to the JDK.
#   - nextLong(bound), nextLong(origin, bound), longs(origin, bound) and nextGaussian(mean, stddev) still
#     use TeaVM's RandomGenerator defaults and differ from the JDK for every seed tried. nextDouble and
#     nextFloat with a bound or a range, ints and doubles matched.
#   - The default seed exists only on Wasm GC (the host import); no other TeaVM backend is built here.
